# Slice 346 (the book, colon-declares and forms-declare-or-call; the author 2026-11-10): what declares and what
# writes. A place is declared by its receiver (`int: i 1`, `@: p Model`) and written by `:=`. A form `Name: tail`
# declares a Structure with the tail inside: an atom naming what answers now is that entity itself -- Holder holds the
# Structure Model, one instance reached through Holder, through the reference p and by its own name; Pair holds the
# place i, so a write through Pair\i is seen by i -- and an atom that answers nothing stays a word (unknown_word; later,
# declared after the form). The repeated declaration of Pair gives its place the new Structure: its fields are counted
# before (2) and after (3), and there is one field Pair.
int: i 1
i := 6
int: i2 i + 1
Model:
    int: v 1
end: Model
@: p Model
Holder: Model unknown_word
Holder\Model\v := 5
int: through_model Model\v
int: through_ref p\v
Pair: a b
int: n_first length(Pair)
Pair: i Model later
int: n_second length(Pair)
Pair\i := 9
int: shared i
int: later 3
